-- 1130-canonicalize-session-part-tool-names.sql.DRAFT — EI-22420642374429444
--
-- PARKED CONTRACT/BACKFILL MIGRATION. Keep the .DRAFT suffix until the
-- catalog-backed writer is deployed and the focused ingest tests are green.
-- The migration runner applies only *.sql files; this draft is deliberately
-- invisible to boot auto-apply while the map and rollout order are verified.
--
-- The session_turn_parts writer historically persisted two client spellings
-- for the same projected MCP tool: Claude's
--   mcp__papercusp-su__<group>_<verb>
-- and Codex's
--   mcp__papercusp_su.<group>_<verb>
-- (Codex also sanitizes hyphens to underscores). Migration 1130 rewrites only
-- aliases observed in the verified corpus AND resolved to a unique canonical
-- name in the live projected catalog used by the companion code fix.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE AUTHORING (2026-09-07, workspace_id='default'):
--   projected MCP catalog: 866 tools (866 colon-form names)
--   wrapper spellings observed: 508 distinct / 382651 rows
--   uniquely catalog-resolved aliases: 472 distinct / 382593 rows
--   deliberately untouched: 36 malformed, unknown, or ambiguous spellings
--
-- This is an EXPLICIT map, not a generic separator normalizer: native tool
-- names, unknown wrappers, malformed parameter-tail values, and separator
-- collisions remain untouched. The workspace scope and fixed 50,000-row pages
-- bound the backfill's mutation and lock footprint while the loop converges.
-- It is idempotent: canonical rows no longer match the map, and re-running
-- after a partial failure resumes from the remaining aliases.
--
-- FORWARD-COMPAT: the currently deployed reader treats tool_name as opaque
-- text, and the new writer emits canonical names while this migration is
-- parked. The old reader therefore remains compatible before/after arming.
-- The migration runner supplies the transaction; do not add BEGIN/COMMIT.

CREATE TEMP TABLE session_part_tool_name_backfill_map (
  alias text PRIMARY KEY,
  canonical text NOT NULL
) ON COMMIT DROP;

